WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.580
"The Recommended Daily
Added Sugar Intake"

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.940
Dating back to the original Dietary Goals
for the United States in 1977—

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.540
the so-called McGovern Report—
leading nutrition scientists

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.780
were not only calling for a
reduction in meat, and other sources

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.940
of saturated fat and cholesterol,
like dairy and eggs, but also sugar.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:31.680
The goal was to reduce
America’s sugar intake down

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.470
to no more than 10% of the daily diet.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:38.350
“The final conclusions would hang sugar,”

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.000
reported the President
of the Sugar Association.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.980
“The McGovern Report has
to be neutralized….”

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:48.650
Don’t worry, though, we have
the National Cattlemen’s Association

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:52.480
on our side and, like Big Sugar,
they appealed to the

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.219
Senate Select Committee
to withdraw the report.

00:00:56.219 --> 00:01:00.329
“The Sugar Industry Empire”
striking back appeared to work.

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:03.260
When the official dietary
guidelines were released in 1980,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:08.180
and then again in ‘85,
no specific limit like 10%,

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.500
just the vague “avoid too much
sugar,” whatever that means.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.590
By ‘95 it got even vaguer:
“Choose a diet moderate in sugars.”

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.500
In 2000, they were at least back to
“limit,” but even that was too strong.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.770
Under pressure from sugar
lobbyists, they went back to

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.630
“moderate your intake of sugars,”
before the 2005 guideline committee

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.000
dropped the s-word completely,
encouraging Americans to

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.530
“Choose carbohydrates
wisely,” whatever that means.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.860
If only there were some kind
of dietary guidelines committee

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.500
that could give us guidance...

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:50.020
The Sugar Association expressed
optimism about that 2005 Committee.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.530
The Sugar Association Incorporated
is “committed to the protection

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.820
and promotion of
[table sugar] consumption,”

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.020
and they will not allow for
the “disparagement of sugar.”

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.680
And, they’re not kidding.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.300
In 2003, the World Health
Organization released a report

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.680
“Diet, nutrition and the
prevention of chronic diseases”

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:11.690
which, for the first time since
the McGovern Report called for

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.140
a reduction in sugar intake to under 10%.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.940
The Sugar Association responded by
threatening to get the U.S.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.250
to withdraw all funding from the WHO.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.240
Here it is, in black and white.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.070
The Sugar Association threatening to
pressure Congress to withdraw funding

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.610
from the World Health
Organization - polio vaccinations,

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:38.760
AIDS medications be damned, you
just don’t mess with the candy man.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.100
The threat was described
as tantamount to blackmail

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:46.640
and worse than any pressure they
had experienced from Big Tobacco.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.290
But now, 15 years later, and 40
years after the first proposed

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:54.200
McGovern Report, the current 2015
to 2020 Dietary Guidelines

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.320
lay out as a key recommendation 
the 10% limit,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.980
currently exceeded by every
age bracket in the United States

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:07.900
starting at age one, with adolescents
averaging 87 grams of sugar a day,

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:13.640
meaning the average teen is effectively
eating 29 sugar packets a day.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.140
The Sugar Association describes
the 10% limit as “extremely low,”

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.870
I mean only like up to
a dozen spoonfuls a day.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:25.390
But, of course, there’s no dietary
requirement for added sugar at all,

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:30.280
and every single calorie we get from
added sugar is a wasted opportunity

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.920
to get calories from sources
that actually provide nutrition

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.420
To the American Heart Association’s
credit, they went further

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.090
trying to push added sugar intake
down to about 6% of calories,

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.740
for which a single can of soda
could send you over the limit,

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:52.720
an added sugar limit exceeded
by 90% of Americans.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.120
In 2017 the American Heart Association
released their guidelines

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:01.200
for children, recommending they get no
more than about 6 teaspoons per day,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.290
which means there’s nearly a
hundred cereals on the U.S. market

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:09.590
for which a single serving exceeded
the entire recommended daily limit.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:14.370
And the heart association recommends
no added sugars at all under age 2,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.600
small toddlers are to avoid
added sugars altogether,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:24.220
a recommendation that’s violated…
in up to 80% of toddlers.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.889
The U.S. is one of at least 65
countries that have implemented

00:04:27.889 --> 00:04:32.430
dietary guidelines or policies
to curb sugar consumption.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.440
In the UK, the Scientific
Advisory Committee on Nutrition

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.970
made new recommendations to
reduce added sugars down to 5%,

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.950
which is the direction the World
Health Organization is headed as well.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:46.030
They always seem to be
ahead of the curve.  Why?

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:48.710
Because their policy-making
process is at least partially

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.810
protected from industry influence.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:55.590
Unlike governments, which may have
competing interests in commerce

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.330
and trade, the World Health Organization

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:02.460
is exclusively concerned with health.

